{
  "id": "Compound::DB00881 - Gene::6565",
  "verbalization": "Quinapril, a clinically used antihypertensive drug, is shown to bind to Slc15a1, a protein involved in transport processes across cellular membranes. Slc15a1 is implicated in multiple transport activities, including proton-dependent peptide transport and proton/oligonucleotide cotransport. These activities are all part of a broader functional role in cellular transport, which is then participated by Slc15a2. The evidence suggests that Quinapril may influence Slc15a2 through its interaction with Slc15a1, potentially modulating the transport function of Slc15a2. The consistent binding of Quinapril to Slc15a1 and the shared participation of Slc15a1 in various transport activities imply a possible functional link between Quinapril and Slc15a2, likely through a mechanism involving the regulation of transport processes. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Slc15a1 as a central mediator in the transport function of Slc15a2, suggesting that Quinapril may exert its effects by interacting with this critical protein.",
